{
  "$schema": "../../scenario.schema.json",
  "id": "reentrancy/an-ai-response-handler-posting-after-a-slower-on-ai-response-hook-stops-at-the-depth-limit",
  "title": "A handler of ai_response that posts into the room after each answer ends at the depth limit when an ASYNC ON_AI_RESPONSE hook slower than the turn delays ai_response past the turn's end",
  "rules": [
    "§8.2",
    "§8.3",
    "§9.2",
    "§10.6.3"
  ],
  "requirements": [
    "RK-8.2-035",
    "RK-8.3-020",
    "RK-8.3-022",
    "RK-8.3-023",
    "RK-8.3-024",
    "RK-8.3-026",
    "RK-8.3-048",
    "RK-9.5.1-054"
  ],
  "notes": "ai_response follows the turn's ON_AI_RESPONSE observers. The hook holds at 'audit' until the inbound call has returned, its turn over: the first ai_response, and the handler's first post, come after the turn, which keeps their handlers at the turn's response depth all the same. The next answers' hooks have no reaction left and return at once. Python: the owner publishes the turn's framework events, during the turn (turns/_owner_publish.py) and after it (turns/_owner_between.py), within its response depth, which core/_kit_wiring.py KitEvents lends each handler while it runs; roomkit-rs: the turn's host emits them with its response depth, which Handlers::emit_at lends each handler. tests/test_reentrance.py::TestACallFromAnAsyncHook::test_a_framework_event_handler_telling_the_agent_again_ends_at_the_depth_limit[ai_response-after a slower ON_AI_RESPONSE hook]. With the turn's floor lent only while the turn ran, that first post was at depth 0 on both implementations.",
  "setup": {
    "channels": [
      {
        "id": "sms",
        "type": "sms",
        "category": "transport"
      },
      {
        "id": "ai",
